1. Controller Responsibilities
Controllers are designed to be lean and focused solely on handling incoming requests
and returning responses. They should act as an orchestration layer, delegating all
business logic to the service layer. The primary responsibility of a controller is to
return a JsonModel object, which encapsulates the service's response.
Example:
[HttpPost]
public async Task<JsonModel> CreateAssessment([FromBody]
CreateHealthAssessmentDto createDto)
{
return await _healthAssessmentService.CreateAssessmentAsync(createDto,
GetToken(HttpContext));
}
2. Service Layer Token Management
Every service method must include a TokenModel parameter. This TokenModel will be
passed from the controller layer using a standardized method, typically
GetToken(HttpContext) from a BaseController .
3. Controller Inheritance
All controllers within the application must inherit from a BaseController . This
ensures consistent behavior, provides common functionalities (like token retrieval),
and enforces architectural patterns across the application.
4. Standardized Response and Error Handling
All API responses, including successful operations and error conditions, must be
encapsulated within a JsonModel . This model should provide a consistent structure
for data, messages, and status codes, facilitating predictable client-side handling.
5. Robust Error Handling in Services
Every service method must implement a try-catch block to gracefully handle
exceptions. In the event of an error, the catch block should return a JsonModel with
an appropriate error message and status code, preventing unhandled exceptions from
propagating to higher layers.
Example:
public JsonModel GetRaisedTicketById(GetTicketReqModel getTicketReqModel,
TokenModel token)
{
try
{
var (userRaisedTickets, totalCount) =
_userRepository.GetUserRaiseTicketById<GetRaisedTicketSPResponseModel>
(token.UserID, getTicketReqModel, token);
var location = _locationService.GetLocationOffsets(token.LocationID,
token);
if (userRaisedTickets == null || !userRaisedTickets.Any())
{
return new JsonModel
{
data = new object(),
Message = "No tickets found for the user",
StatusCode = (int)HttpStatusCodes.NoContent
};
}
var options = new JsonSerializerOptions { PropertyNameCaseInsensitive =
true };
var getRaisedTicketByIdResponceModelsList =
userRaisedTickets.Select(ticket => new GetRaisedTicketByIdResponseModel
{
CreatedDate = ConvertFromUtcTimeNew((DateTime)ticket.CreatedDate,
location, token),
Remarks = ticket.Remarks,
Priority = ticket.Priority,
Status = ticket.Status,
CategoryName = ticket.CategoryName,
Description = ticket.Description,
TicketId = ticket.TicketId,
CreatedBy = ticket.CreatedBy,
UpdatedBy = ticket.UpdatedBy,
UpdatedDate = ticket.UpdatedDate,
IsActive = ticket.IsActive,
CreatorName = ticket.CreatorName,
CreatorRole = ticket.CreatorRole,
RoleName = ticket.RoleName,
Files = ticket.Files != null ? DeserializeTicketFiles(ticket.Files,
options, token, location) : null,
ResolvedDate = ticket.ResolvedDate != null ?
(DateTime?)ConvertFromUtcTimeNew((DateTime)ticket.ResolvedDate, location,
token) : null,
}).ToList();
return new JsonModel
{
data = getRaisedTicketByIdResponceModelsList,
Message = "Retrieved all user tickets",
meta = new Meta()
{
TotalRecords = totalCount,
PageSize = getTicketReqModel.PageSize,
CurrentPage = getTicketReqModel.PageNumber,
TotalPages = Math.Ceiling(Convert.ToDecimal(totalCount /
getTicketReqModel.PageSize)),
DefaultPageSize = getTicketReqModel.PageSize
},
StatusCode = (int)HttpStatusCodes.OK
};
}
catch (Exception e)
{
return new JsonModel
{
data = new object(),
Message = e.Message,
StatusCode = (int)HttpStatusCodes.InternalServerError
};
}
}
6. Base Entity Inheritance
All core entity classes within the domain model should inherit from a BaseEntity
class. This provides a common foundation for properties such as Id , CreatedDate ,
UpdatedDate , etc., ensuring consistency and simplifying data management
operations.
7. TokenModel Parameter Handling
The TokenModel parameter in service methods should generally not be optional and
should not be assigned a default value of null . It should typically be the last
parameter in the method signature. Avoid providing default values to parameters
unless absolutely necessary, to maintain clarity and enforce explicit parameter
passing.
